<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Transpadane Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1796 CE – 1797 CE · capital Milan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A French-sponsored republic in Lombardy, created after Napoleon's conquest of Milan in 1796 and the first of the Italian sister republics.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After defeating the Austrians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> in May 1796, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> entered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Milan</a> and ended Austrian rule in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. The French set up an administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobin-club">Jacobins</a> and moderates, and in November 1796 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> north of the Po was organised as the Transpadane Republic.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was governed under French military control, with local administrations in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Milan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> cities. It paid heavy war contributions and saw church and noble property seized and artworks shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, but also had a lively political press and debate about Italian unity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan's</a> patriots organised a competition in 1796 on the best form of free government for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In June and July 1797 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> merged the Transpadane Republic with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cispadane-republic">Cispadane Republic</a> and other territories to form the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cisalpine-republic">Cisalpine Republic</a>, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Milan</a> as capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/transpadane-republic.jpg" alt="Transpadane Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: PavelD · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Repubblica_Transpadana.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1796 CE – 1797 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Milan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, Lombard, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Repubblica Transpadana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1796&amp;focus=transpadane-republic" aria-label="Transpadane Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4898 574 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Transpadane Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5218 830L5218 830M5218 830L5054 728M5218 830L5218 830"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5218 830h0M5054 728h0M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Milan. <a href="/atlas?year=1796&amp;focus=transpadane-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
